1
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,320
On 25 January 1971, while
Uganda's president, Milton Obote,

2
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:17,760
was at a Commonwealth
conference in Singapore,

3
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:21,660
soldiers sealed Entebbe airport
and took control of Kampala.

4
00:00:22,229 --> 00:00:25,374
The man behind it was the army
commander, General Idi Amin.

5
00:00:25,990 --> 00:00:30,655
Estimates by human rights organisations and
legal bodies run from one hundred thousand

6
00:00:30,655 --> 00:00:33,895
to five hundred thousand
Ugandans killed under his rule,

7
00:00:34,315 --> 00:00:36,455
and no one has produced an exact count.

8
00:00:36,994 --> 00:00:39,194
He lived for twenty-four
years after losing power,

9
00:00:39,634 --> 00:00:40,634
and never stood trial.

10
00:00:41,181 --> 00:00:44,086
This film uses official
records, commissions of inquiry,

11
00:00:44,446 --> 00:00:46,586
and archive footage that
shows real violence.

12
00:00:47,278 --> 00:00:51,278
Amin was born in the 1920s, in what
was then a British protectorate,

13
00:00:51,618 --> 00:00:53,498
and the exact year is still disputed.

14
00:00:54,157 --> 00:00:57,157
He joined the colonial regiment,
the King's African Rifles,

15
00:00:57,577 --> 00:01:02,157
in 1946, and rose through the
ranks of the new Ugandan army.

16
00:01:02,727 --> 00:01:08,912
By 1970 he and Obote had fallen out, and
accounts say Obote was preparing to investigate

17
00:01:08,912 --> 00:01:10,032
him over army funds.

18
00:01:10,703 --> 00:01:16,068
On the radio, the new rulers accused Obote of
corruption and of favouring some regions over others.

19
00:01:16,766 --> 00:01:20,951
Loyal soldiers resisted in Jinja
and at the Kampala police college,

20
00:01:20,951 --> 00:01:24,611
but by the afternoon the army said
it controlled the whole country.

21
00:01:25,196 --> 00:01:30,876
Many Ugandans welcomed the change, and on
2 February Amin declared himself president,

22
00:01:31,176 --> 00:01:33,496
promising an early
return to civilian rule.

23
00:01:34,049 --> 00:01:37,309
Within months, soldiers from
the Acholi and Lango peoples,

24
00:01:37,709 --> 00:01:42,589
seen as loyal to Obote, were being killed
in army barracks at Mbarara and Jinja.

25
00:01:43,159 --> 00:01:49,679
A United States embassy telegram of 27 June
1973 reports that a soldier convicted of

26
00:01:49,679 --> 00:01:52,599
murder was shot by firing
squad in public at Tororo,

27
00:01:53,059 --> 00:01:56,179
and that the execution was filmed
and shown on local television.

28
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:01,560
A Ugandan historian, Dr Vincent Magombe,
remembers the first firing squad.

29
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:23,940
On 4 August 1972, Amin announced
what he called an economic war.

30
00:02:24,487 --> 00:02:27,787
About eighty thousand people of
South Asian descent lived in Uganda,

31
00:02:28,227 --> 00:02:31,287
and many of them ran its
shops, mills and businesses.

32
00:02:32,110 --> 00:02:34,850
He ordered those who held
British passports to leave,

33
00:02:35,170 --> 00:02:40,130
and days later widened the order to cover
other Asians who were not Ugandan citizens.

34
00:02:40,862 --> 00:02:43,647
They were given ninety
days, until 8 November.

35
00:02:54,067 --> 00:02:56,247
About twenty-seven thousand
people went to Britain,

36
00:02:57,047 --> 00:03:00,047
around six thousand to Canada,
and thousands more to India,

37
00:03:00,307 --> 00:03:01,227
Kenya and elsewhere.

38
00:03:01,878 --> 00:03:04,618
Many of the families had lived
in Uganda for generations,

39
00:03:05,058 --> 00:03:07,758
and India broke off diplomatic
relations in protest.

40
00:03:08,465 --> 00:03:12,405
Uganda's government confiscated more
than five thousand six hundred firms,

41
00:03:12,765 --> 00:03:16,805
ranches and farms, and gave most
of them to soldiers and officials.

42
00:03:17,530 --> 00:03:22,170
By some estimates, Asians had owned about
ninety percent of the country's businesses,

43
00:03:22,710 --> 00:03:28,490
and manufacturing output fell by about
two thirds between 1972 and 1979.

44
00:03:29,225 --> 00:03:31,990
A secret American intelligence
briefing for the President,

45
00:03:32,510 --> 00:03:38,090
dated 23 August 1972, called Amin's
behaviour increasingly irrational,

46
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:41,430
and warned that the expulsion
would damage the economy.

47
00:03:42,071 --> 00:03:45,376
That year, the chief
justice, Benedicto Kiwanuka,

48
00:03:45,716 --> 00:03:48,516
who had been Uganda's first
prime minister, was killed.

49
00:03:54,095 --> 00:03:59,460
To the outside world, Amin was a showman,
and in December 1973 he launched a fund to

50
00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:01,220
save Britain from its economic crisis.

51
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:15,640
In 1974 Amin himself set up a commission of
inquiry into disappearances since the coup.

52
00:04:16,212 --> 00:04:22,272
Its report, handed over in June 1975, examined
three hundred and eight cases and blamed

53
00:04:22,272 --> 00:04:25,192
the State Research Bureau
and the Public Safety Unit,

54
00:04:25,632 --> 00:04:27,652
though it did not find
that Amin gave the orders.

55
00:04:28,390 --> 00:04:30,980
Amin rejected the report,
and it was never published.

56
00:04:31,596 --> 00:04:38,561
On 27 June 1976, an Air France jet from Tel
Aviv to Paris was hijacked after leaving

57
00:04:38,561 --> 00:04:43,261
Athens, by two Palestinians and
two Germans, and flown to Entebbe.

58
00:04:51,995 --> 00:04:56,380
The hijackers demanded five million dollars
and the release of fifty-three prisoners,

59
00:04:56,860 --> 00:05:00,120
and threatened to begin
killing hostages on 1 July.

60
00:05:00,976 --> 00:05:06,281
Reports say that Amin welcomed the hijackers, and
Ugandan soldiers helped to guard the hostages.

61
00:05:07,073 --> 00:05:10,098
More than a hundred passengers who
were not Israeli were released,

62
00:05:10,658 --> 00:05:15,138
the deadline was extended to 4 July,
and about a hundred hostages remained.

63
00:05:15,851 --> 00:05:21,311
Late on 3 July, Israeli commandos landed at
Entebbe and freed one hundred and two hostages

64
00:05:21,311 --> 00:05:22,491
in about an hour.

65
00:05:23,030 --> 00:05:26,015
Three hostages died, along
with the Israeli commander,

66
00:05:26,275 --> 00:05:31,175
Yonatan Netanyahu, all seven hijackers,
and about forty-five Ugandan soldiers.

67
00:05:31,988 --> 00:05:37,268
One hostage, Dora Bloch, an elderly woman,
had been taken to a Kampala hospital before

68
00:05:37,268 --> 00:05:39,268
the raid, and she was killed afterwards.

69
00:05:39,987 --> 00:05:44,992
Amin's former health minister, Henry Kyemba,
later testified that it was on Amin's orders.

70
00:05:45,654 --> 00:05:49,039
Wycliffe Kato, who worked in
air traffic control at Entebbe,

71
00:05:49,259 --> 00:05:55,139
said he was arrested in 1976 and taken to the
State Research Bureau's headquarters at Nakasero.

72
00:06:08,462 --> 00:06:13,647
On 16 February 1977, Archbishop
Janani Luwum was arrested,

73
00:06:14,067 --> 00:06:16,347
and the government said
he died in a car crash.

74
00:06:17,047 --> 00:06:20,747
A week later the House of Lords debated
Uganda's place in the Commonwealth.

75
00:06:21,311 --> 00:06:25,126
Amin's own health minister wrote that the
bodies he saw were riddled with bullets.

76
00:06:25,837 --> 00:06:32,082
In June 1977 the Commonwealth's leaders
condemned the massive violation of basic human

77
00:06:32,082 --> 00:06:33,022
rights in Uganda.

78
00:06:33,699 --> 00:06:35,684
No one knows how many people died.

79
00:06:36,270 --> 00:06:41,670
In 1977 the International Commission of Jurists
said that no reliable estimate was possible,

80
00:06:41,930 --> 00:06:45,870
but that many sources believed the total
was well over one hundred thousand.

81
00:06:46,499 --> 00:06:51,404
In 1978, Amnesty International told a United
States Senate committee that it was about

82
00:06:51,404 --> 00:06:56,284
three hundred thousand, and other estimates run
from one hundred thousand to five hundred thousand.

83
00:06:56,890 --> 00:07:02,475
In October 1978, Amin's troops crossed into
Tanzania and occupied the Kagera region,

84
00:07:02,855 --> 00:07:04,495
and historians still debate why.

85
00:07:05,031 --> 00:07:10,156
Kampala fell on 11 April 1979,
and Amin fled by helicopter.

86
00:07:24,895 --> 00:07:28,000
Yusuf Lule, of the Uganda
National Liberation Front,

87
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:30,320
was installed as president two days later.

88
00:07:30,839 --> 00:07:34,959
Amin went first to Libya, and
then, in 1980, to Saudi Arabia,

89
00:07:35,299 --> 00:07:37,939
which gave him a home on condition
that he stay out of politics.

90
00:07:38,693 --> 00:07:44,373
In 1986 Uganda's new government opened a
commission of inquiry into human rights violations

91
00:07:44,373 --> 00:07:47,073
between 1962 and 1986.

92
00:07:47,813 --> 00:07:51,793
It heard six hundred and eight witnesses,
and its seven hundred and twenty page report,

93
00:07:52,053 --> 00:07:54,933
delivered in 1994, was never made public.

94
00:07:55,625 --> 00:08:00,570
On 16 August 2003, Idi Amin
died in a hospital in Jeddah,

95
00:08:00,610 --> 00:08:03,579
after weeks in intensive care,
believed to be seventy-eight.

96
00:08:04,024 --> 00:08:07,129
He was buried in Jeddah,
and no court ever tried him.

97
00:08:07,598 --> 00:08:12,463
Amnesty International called his death a
sad comment on the world's inability to hold

98
00:08:12,463 --> 00:08:13,423
leaders to account.

99
00:08:14,383 --> 00:08:16,263
No court ever tried Idi Amin.

100
00:08:16,783 --> 00:08:17,943
Was justice ever done?
